WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.616
This self join uses admissions twice so we can
compare an earlier

00:00:03.642 --> 00:00:09.154
encounter with a later encounter for the same
patient The patient IDs

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.748
must match We also require the first admission ID to
be lower

00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:16.419
than the second so the pair has a direction and we
avoid

00:00:16.446 --> 00:00:22.155
reverse duplicates After joining the two admission
aliases we join

00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:26.473
patients to retrieve the patient name The WHERE
clause keeps pairs where

00:00:26.487 --> 00:00:29.881
the first admission has a discharge date and the
later admission begins

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:35.346
between one and ninety days afterward Run the query
and inspect the

00:00:35.359 --> 00:00:40.893
days-between calculation This is a good example of
why aliases

00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:45.514
are more than a formatting convenience The aliases
give the two roles

00:00:45.527 --> 00:00:51.344
clear meanings earlier admission and later admission
When you build self joins

00:00:51.594 --> 00:00:54.686
define those roles in plain language before writing
the SQL
